NEON for Python
***************

<div class=perex>

The package `nette-neon` reads and writes NEON in Python, for example your `config.neon` or the `phpstan.neon` of a PHP project. With it you:

- decode a configuration file into Python values and encode values back to NEON
- [edit a file |editing] while every comment and space you did not touch stays where it was
- check files with `neon-lint` in your editor, in CI or after an AI agent

</div>


Installation
============

```shell
pip install nette-neon
```

Or `uv add nette-neon`. The package is imported as `neon`. It requires Python 3.13 or newer, it is fully typed and has no dependencies.


Decoding and Encoding
=====================

The function `decode()` turns NEON into Python values, `decode_file()` does the same for a file, and `encode()` turns values back:

```python
from neon import decode, decode_file, encode

decode("roles: [guest, admin]")              # {'roles': ['guest', 'admin']}
config = decode_file("config.neon")
encode({"roles": ["guest", "admin"]})        # '{roles: [guest, admin]}'
encode({"roles": ["guest", "admin"]}, True)  # 'roles:\n\t- guest\n\t- admin\n\n'
```

Instead of `True`, the second argument of `encode()` can be a function that decides for each array whether to write it as a block. It gets the array and the list of keys on the way to it:

```python
encode(config, lambda value, path: not path)  # blocks for the first level only
```

The values of the [format] become these Python values:

| NEON | Python
|-----------------------------------------
| `null`, `true`, `yes` … | `None`, `bool`
| integer | `int`, of any size
| float | `float`
| string | `str`
| [date |format#Dates] | `neon.Date`
| [entity |format#Entities], chain | `neon.Entity`
| sequence | `list`
| mapping | `dict` with string keys, in the order of the file

The mapping is one-to-one: `1.0` stays a `float`, a large integer stays exact, `{}` is a `dict` and `[]` a `list`. A `neon.Date` holds the date as it was written, including a missing time zone; `to_datetime()` turns it into a `datetime`. When encoding, `datetime` and `date` are accepted as well.


Errors
======

When the input is not valid NEON, `decode()` and `parse()` raise `neon.NeonError`. Its message says where the problem is, and its attribute `position` holds the line and the column:

```python
from neon import NeonError, decode

try:
    decode("database:\n\thost: localhost\n\thost: db")
except NeonError as e:
    print(e)                # Duplicated key 'host' on line 3 at column 2
    print(e.position.line)  # 3
```


Editing Files
=============

The function `parse()` gives the syntax tree of a file, and through it you change the file so that the rest of it stays as its author wrote it:

```python
from pathlib import Path
from neon import parse

file = Path("config.neon")
doc = parse(file.read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
doc.set_value(["database", "host"], "db.example.com")
file.write_text(doc.print(), encoding="utf-8")
```

How to change, add, remove and rename items, and how to find your way around the tree, is described on the page [Editing Files |editing].


Checking Files: neon-lint
=========================

The package comes with the command `neon-lint`. Give it files or directories, and it prints one line for each problem:

```shell
neon-lint config/
```

```
config/local.neon:3:2: Duplicated key 'user'
```

The command, its options and its exit codes are the same as in the [PHP library |php#Checking Files: neon-lint]. To run it without installing the package, use `uvx --from nette-neon neon-lint config/`.

An AI agent that runs it after every change of a `.neon` file learns about a broken file right away. The [guide for AI agents |https://github.com/nette/neon-py/blob/master/docs/skills/agents.md] shows how to set it up.

More examples, each with its real output, are in the [repository of the library |https://github.com/nette/neon-py/tree/master/examples].

